window.NOBEL_INDEX = [{"p":"Overview","f":"index.html","h":"read","t":"What to read","lv":2,"k":"Four documents. Read them in order if this is your first pass. 01 — Product Product Requirements What we are building and why, in plain language. Who it is for, what happens day to day, what counts as success, and what we are deliberately not doing. Read the PRD → 02 — Engineering System Architecture How audio gets from a phone in a store to a feedback message. Components, the recording pipeline, the processing pipeline, failure handling, security. Read the architecture → 03 — Engineering Data Model & API Database tables, storage layout, API contracts between app and backend, the rubric format, and the exact prompt structure for scoring. Read the data model → 04 — Planning Roadmap & Risks Phased delivery plan, the open questions that need answers before we commit, honest risk register, and owner-by-owner next steps. Read the roadmap →"},{"p":"Overview","f":"index.html","h":"problem","t":"The problem","lv":2,"k":"In e-commerce, every step of a sale is measured. In a physical store, almost none of it is. A retailer knows what was sold and what the footfall was. They do not know what the associate actually said, whether they asked the customer what they needed, whether they knew the product, or whether they even greeted the person who walked in. Today the only tool is a manager standing nearby, or a mystery shopper visiting once a quarter. Both are rare, subjective, and too late to change anything. So coaching in retail is mostly opinion delivered occasionally, and nobody can tell whether it worked. The one-line version Retail has no equivalent of a call recording. We are trying to create one, and then make it useful to the person who was on the call."},{"p":"Overview","f":"index.html","h":"shape","t":"Shape of the system","lv":2,"k":"Five moving parts. Everything in these documents is an elaboration of this diagram. Record Phone in the associate's pocket Upload Batched, on Wi-Fi, every ~2 hours Transcribe Speech to text, split by speaker Score LLM against the retailer's rubric Deliver Daily nudge, weekly manager view React Native Opus + S3 Voicemaker LLM + rubric App / WhatsApp End-to-end flow. Nothing is scored on the device; the phone only records and uploads."},{"p":"Overview","f":"index.html","h":"bets","t":"Bets we are making","lv":2,"k":"These are assumptions, not facts. Each one is something the pilot is designed to test. If any of the first three is wrong, the product does not work in its current form. #The betIf it is wrongConfidence 1 A phone mic in a pocket or lanyard picks up the customer clearly enough to transcribe. Whole approach needs external mics. Cost and rollout friction go up sharply. Low 2 Transcription is accurate enough in mixed Hindi–English retail speech to score fairly. Scores become noise. Associates stop trusting the feedback within a week. Medium 3 Associates will keep the app running rather than quietly killing it. Coverage collapses. We measure only the people who already comply. Medium 4 An LLM can apply a retail rubric about as consistently as a decent human manager. Fall back to a much narrower rubric: presence/absence checks only, no quality judgement. High 5 Daily specific feedback actually"},{"p":"Overview","f":"index.html","h":"numbers","t":"Numbers that matter","lv":2,"k":"Rough planning figures for a single-store pilot. Derivations are in the architecture document. Figures marked &dagger; are placeholders They were drafted to make the shape of the problem concrete, not derived from measurement or agreed with the customer. Nothing marked &dagger; should be quoted externally or planned against until it has been checked. Hover any marker to see this note again. ~11 MB&dagger; Audio per associate per shift 8h · Opus 16 kbps mono ~4&dagger; Uploads per shift every 2h, Wi-Fi preferred < 30 min&dagger; Shift end to feedback target, not guaranteed 15 – 25&dagger; Conversations per associate/day assumption, to be measured"},{"p":"Overview","f":"index.html","h":"decided","t":"Decided vs. open","lv":2,"k":"Decided React Native for the app, chosen for development speed, not cross-platform reach. Android only for Phase 1. iOS's background-recording limits are a harder problem; deferred, not ruled out. Supabase for backend, auth, database, storage and functions. Voicemaker for transcription, flagship model. Server-side diarization. Nothing about speaker separation on the device. No on-device transcription. Quantized models lose too much accuracy. Hindi and English for the first deployment. Phone mic only in Phase 1. External hardware is Phase 2. The retailer writes the rubric. We supply a starting template, they own it. Open Do we keep the audio after transcription, or only the transcript? Continuous vs. triggered recording — always-on, or associate taps to start? Consent mechanism for the customer. Signage alone, or something explicit? Feedback channel — in-app, WhatsApp, or both from day on"},{"p":"Overview","f":"index.html","h":"team","t":"Who does what","lv":2,"k":"PersonOwnsImmediate next step Sabari System architecture, mobile app, backend pipeline This document set, then the RN background-recording spike Sujit Transcription stack, Voicemaker integration Enable multi-channel diarization; share API docs with Sabari Yatin Customer relationship, rubric definition, pilot site Set up the three-way group; get rubric v1 from the retailer How to use these docs Nothing here is final. Where a decision is genuinely undecided it says so, rather than picking something to look complete. Argue with the bets in section 04 first — those matter more than any implementation detail. Next Product Requirements →"},{"p":"PRD","f":"prd.html","h":"why","t":"Why this exists","lv":2,"k":"Imagine two people selling the same phone in the same store on the same day. One sells nine. The other sells two. Everyone assumes the first person is just better. Nobody can say what they actually did differently, because nobody was listening. That is the entire problem. In a physical store, the most important thing that happens — the conversation between an associate and a customer — leaves no record. It happens, it affects whether a sale occurs, and then it evaporates. Because there is no record, three things follow: Coaching is guesswork A manager coaches based on what they happened to overhear, or on a gut feeling about someone. It is not wrong, exactly. It is just unevidenced. Good habits do not spread When someone finds a line that works, it stays with them. There is no mechanism to notice it, name it, and teach it to the other eleven people on the floor. Training is unmeasurable "},{"p":"PRD","f":"prd.html","h":"goal","t":"The end goal","lv":2,"k":"In one sentence Every retail associate should finish their shift knowing one specific thing they did well and one specific thing to do differently tomorrow — based on what they actually said, not on someone's impression of them. That is the whole product. Everything else is machinery in service of that sentence. Three things have to be true for it to be worth building: The feedback has to be specific. \"Improve your product knowledge\" is useless. \"A customer asked about battery life twice and you moved to price both times\" is something a person can act on. It has to arrive quickly. Feedback about Tuesday delivered on Friday is a performance review. Feedback about Tuesday delivered on Tuesday evening is coaching. The associate has to not hate it. If it feels like surveillance, they will find a way to defeat it, and they will be right to. It has to feel like something that helps them earn m"},{"p":"PRD","f":"prd.html","h":"who","t":"Who uses it","lv":2,"k":"PersonWhat they wantWhat they getWhat worries them The associatePrimary user To sell more, hit incentives, not be embarrassed in front of colleagues A short private message at the end of each shift: one strength, one fix \"Is this going to be used to fire me?\" The store managerSecondary To know who needs help this week without hovering over everyone A weekly view of the team, sorted by who is struggling and with what \"Is this more admin work for me?\" The regional / L&D headBuyer To prove training spend produces behaviour change Trend lines across stores, before and after training interventions \"Will this create a legal problem for us?\" Note the tension The person who buys this and the person who uses it are not the same, and they do not want the same thing. The buyer wants oversight. The user wants help. If we build purely for the buyer we get a compliance tool that associates sabotage. T"},{"p":"PRD","f":"prd.html","h":"day","t":"A day in the life","lv":2,"k":"Concretely, here is what the product looks like from the associate's side. 09:45 — Shift start Ravi clocks in and opens the app One tap on \"Start shift\". The app confirms it is recording with a persistent notification. He puts the phone in his shirt pocket and forgets about it. 10:00 – 18:00 — On the floor He works a normal day He talks to maybe twenty customers. He takes a lunch break and pauses recording with one tap. Roughly every two hours, when the store Wi-Fi is reachable, the app quietly sends what it has recorded. He does not notice this happening. 18:00 — Shift end He taps \"End shift\" Recording stops. Anything not yet uploaded goes now. Phone is at 38%, which is survivable but not great. 18:20 — Feedback A message arrives \"Today you had 18 conversations. In 15 of them you asked what the customer would mainly use the phone for — that is your strongest habit and it is above the st"},{"p":"PRD","f":"prd.html","h":"does","t":"What the product does","lv":2,"k":"Broken into the five things it must do, in order. 5.1 — Record the conversation The app records audio while the associate is on shift. Not video, not location, just audio, and only between \"start shift\" and \"end shift\". The associate can pause at any time and the app makes it obvious when it is recording. Recording must survive the phone being locked and put in a pocket, which is the single hardest technical requirement in the product. 5.2 — Turn it into text Audio goes to our servers and is converted to a transcript. The transcript needs to mark who said what — associate or customer — because \"did the associate ask a question\" is meaningless if we cannot tell who was speaking. Conversations happen in a mix of Hindi and English, frequently within the same sentence, which is normal in Indian retail and difficult for speech systems. 5.3 — Split it into conversations Eight hours of audio is"},{"p":"PRD","f":"prd.html","h":"rubric","t":"What \"good\" means","lv":2,"k":"The retailer defines this, not us We do not know what good selling looks like in a jewellery store versus a phone store versus a pharmacy. The retailer does. We provide a starting template and the tools to edit it. They own the definition. This is a deliberate product decision, not laziness — a rubric the retailer did not write is a rubric their managers will not defend. A rubric is a list of things to check for. A reasonable starting template for electronics retail: BehaviourWhat we look for in the transcriptWeight Greeting Did the associate speak first, within roughly the first 30 seconds, with a warm opening rather than \"yes?\" 10%&dagger; Needs discovery Did they ask what the customer actually needs — use case, budget, who it is for — before recommending anything? 25%&dagger; Product knowledge Were the customer's questions answered accurately and confidently, or deflected? 20%&dagger;"},{"p":"PRD","f":"prd.html","h":"feedback","t":"The feedback itself","lv":2,"k":"This is the part of the product that people actually experience, so it deserves more care than the pipeline that produces it. Rules Under 80 words. It is read on a bus. Exactly one strength and one improvement. Not three of each. Quote what they actually said. The improvement includes what to say instead. Compare to their own past, not to the top performer. Neutral tone. Not a cheerleader, not a manager. Never A bare number with no explanation. A ranking against named colleagues. Anything that reads as a warning or a threat. Feedback on a day with too little usable audio — say nothing instead. Comment on accent, personality, or anything not in the rubric. More than one message per day. Delivery channel WhatsApp is where these users already are, and open rates will be far higher than an app notification. It also means the associate does not need to open our app to get value from it, which"},{"p":"PRD","f":"prd.html","h":"manager","t":"The manager view","lv":2,"k":"A weekly dashboard, not a live one. A live dashboard invites a manager to watch people in real time, which turns the product into surveillance and destroys the associate's trust in it. The manager should be able to answer four questions in under a minute: Who on my team is improving and who is sliding? What is the one behaviour my whole team is weakest on this week? Who has enough data for me to trust these numbers at all? What should I say in Monday's huddle? Coverage must be visible If an associate only produced two usable conversations this week, their score is meaningless and the dashboard must say so loudly rather than showing a number. The fastest way to lose the manager's trust is to let them act on a score built from noise."},{"p":"PRD","f":"prd.html","h":"success","t":"What success looks like","lv":2,"k":"Figures marked &dagger; are placeholders They were drafted to make the shape of the problem concrete, not derived from measurement or agreed with the customer. Nothing marked &dagger; should be quoted externally or planned against until it has been checked. Hover any marker to see this note again. For the pilot, we are not measuring revenue. Attributing a sales change to a coaching tool over a few weeks in one store is not credible, and claiming it would damage our own case. We are measuring whether the machine works and whether people use it. QuestionMeasurePilot target Does it capture anything usable?Share of recorded audio that yields a scoreable conversation> 60%&dagger; Do associates keep it on?Recorded hours ÷ scheduled shift hours> 70%&dagger; Is the transcription good enough?Word error rate on a hand-checked sample< 25%&dagger; Do the scores mean anything?Agreement between system"},{"p":"PRD","f":"prd.html","h":"notdoing","t":"What we are not doing","lv":2,"k":"Explicitly out of scope for the first version. Listed so nobody assumes otherwise. Not doingWhy not Live coaching during a conversationRequires real-time transcription, which our provider does not offer yet. Also questionable — an earpiece telling you what to say makes you worse at talking to people. Attributing sales to conversationsNeeds POS integration and a way to link a specific conversation to a specific transaction. Real, valuable, and a whole project on its own. Video or body languageEnormously more intrusive for a marginal signal. Emotion or sentiment scoringUnreliable across cultures and accents, and easy to weaponise against people. Deliberately excluded. Dedicated hardware or lapel micsPhase 2, and only if the phone mic proves inadequate — which it might. Languages beyond Hindi and EnglishLater. Get the pipeline right on two first. Automated performance managementNobody gets "},{"p":"PRD","f":"prd.html","h":"privacy","t":"Recording people","lv":2,"k":"We are recording customers who did not download our app and have no relationship with us. This is the part most likely to become a problem, and it deserves to be handled properly rather than deferred. What we will do for the pilot Visible notice at the store entrance stating that conversations may be recorded for training and quality purposes. Same principle as CCTV signage, in English and the local language. Associates sign a consent form and can pause recording at any moment with no justification required. Personal details get stripped from transcripts before storage — phone numbers, names, card numbers, addresses. Transcripts are deleted after 90 days. Only aggregate scores persist. Nobody browses recordings. Managers see scores and summaries, not raw transcripts, except in a specific dispute where the associate asks for review. Honest position India's DPDP Act creates real obligation"},{"p":"PRD","f":"prd.html","h":"fail","t":"How this could fail","lv":2,"k":"The five most likely ways this does not work, stated plainly. 1 The audio is unusable A phone in a shirt pocket picks up the associate's chest resonance clearly and the customer, standing four feet away in a noisy store, barely at all. Since half the rubric depends on the customer's side, this breaks everything. Most likely failure mode. 2 Nobody keeps it running Battery drain, forgetting to start, or quiet resistance to being recorded. Coverage drops to the point where the data represents only the most compliant staff. 3 The scores feel wrong An associate reads feedback about a conversation they remember going well, disagrees, and stops believing any of it. Trust is lost once and does not come back. 4 It becomes a stick Despite our commitments, a regional manager uses the scores in an appraisal. Word spreads across stores in days and the tool is dead as a coaching product. 5 It works bu"},{"p":"Architecture","f":"architecture.html","h":"scope","t":"What this covers","lv":2,"k":"The PRD says what we are building and why. This document says how — the actual components, the data flowing between them, and the places most likely to break in a real store rather than in a demo. If you are deciding whether a technical choice is sound, this is the document to argue with. Table and API specifics live in Data Model & API so this document can stay about shape and reasoning rather than field names."},{"p":"Architecture","f":"architecture.html","h":"glance","t":"Components at a glance","lv":2,"k":"Six pieces. Nothing on the device beyond recording, compressing, and uploading — every judgement happens server-side. Mobile app React Native · Android records · compresses uploads Opus chunks Supabase Storage + Edge Fns auth · object store triggers pipeline Voicemaker Transcription API speech → text speaker diarization Segmentation Edge function shift audio → conversations Scoring LLM Rubric-based grading score + quote + justification Postgres Shifts · conversations · rubrics · scores — read and written by every stage above single source of truth Delivery App / WhatsApp daily nudge · weekly view Device Backend · third party Output Solid arrows are data moving forward through the pipeline. Dashed lines mean that stage reads from or writes to Postgres, which underlies everything but drives nothing directly — it is the record of truth, not a processing step."},{"p":"Architecture","f":"architecture.html","h":"mobile","t":"Mobile app & recording","lv":2,"k":"The single hardest requirement in the whole system lives here: recording audio continuously for an entire shift, while the phone is locked, in a pocket, doing nothing else the associate would notice. Why React Native, and Android only Two separate decisions worth keeping distinct. React Native was chosen for development speed — it is the framework we can iterate fastest in for a pilot, not a choice made to get iOS and Android from one codebase. Android is the only target for Phase 1. iOS's stricter limits on continuous background audio — session suspension under memory pressure, tighter background-execution budgets — make an 8-hour always-recording session a much harder and less predictable problem there than on Android. Rather than split effort solving that on both platforms at once, Phase 1 ships Android only. iOS is deferred, not ruled out; the same React Native codebase can extend to"},{"p":"Architecture","f":"architecture.html","h":"upload","t":"Upload & chunking","lv":2,"k":"The app does not hold eight hours of audio and upload it all at shift end. It uploads in chunks roughly every two hours, for three reasons: it caps how much is lost if the app crashes or the phone dies, it keeps individual uploads small enough to survive patchy store Wi-Fi, and it lets transcription start before the shift is even over. Trigger: time-based (every ~2h) and opportunistic (on reaching known store Wi-Fi), not continuous streaming. Payload: a single chunk is roughly 2-3 MB Opus, far under Voicemaker's ~100 MB per-request limit, leaving headroom rather than sitting near the ceiling. Retry: exponential backoff, resumable — a failed chunk is retried on the next window, not abandoned. Ordering: chunks carry a sequence number and shift ID so segmentation (section 7) can reconstruct a continuous timeline even if chunks arrive out of order. The polling interval is tunable per deploym"},{"p":"Architecture","f":"architecture.html","h":"backend","t":"Backend on Supabase","lv":2,"k":"Supabase gives us Postgres, auth, object storage, and serverless functions in one place, which matters for a three-person team trying to reach a pilot quickly rather than run infrastructure. Nothing here is exotic enough to justify assembling the equivalent ourselves. What Supabase does for us Auth for associates and managers, with row-level security scoping every query to a store Object storage for raw audio chunks and, if we keep it, transcript text Postgres as the single source of truth for shifts, conversations, rubrics, and scores Edge functions as the glue: triggered on upload, they call Voicemaker, then segmentation, then the scoring LLM What we are watching Edge function cold starts and timeout limits under a full processing chain — may need to split into a queue rather than one long function Storage egress cost if audio retention turns out to be long — see retention decision Row"},{"p":"Architecture","f":"architecture.html","h":"transcribe","t":"Transcription & diarization","lv":2,"k":"Each uploaded chunk goes to Voicemaker's flagship model. Two things matter more than raw accuracy: telling associate from customer, and handling code-mixed Hindi-English speech, which is the normal register of retail conversation in most Indian cities. Diarization is not yet enabled on Voicemaker's side for multi-channel input — Sujit is enabling it. Until then, transcripts have text but not a reliable speaker label, which blocks most of the rubric. Language: Hindi and English for the first deployment. Dialectal variation — Punjabi-accented Hindi is the flagged example — is an accuracy gap we have not measured yet. Real-time is not available. Transcription happens after a chunk is fully uploaded, which is consistent with feedback being end-of-shift rather than live, and matches the product decision to never coach mid-conversation. No on-device transcription. Ruled out on the expectation "},{"p":"Architecture","f":"architecture.html","h":"segment","t":"Conversation segmentation","lv":2,"k":"A shift transcript is a wall of text spanning eight hours. Segmentation cuts it into individual customer conversations — the unit the rubric actually scores. Signal: silence gaps above a threshold, diarization turn-taking patterns, and a minimum-length filter to discard stray small talk between colleagues. Failure modes to design against: merging two customers into one scored conversation (dilutes and confuses the rubric), or splitting one customer into two (scores an incomplete interaction as if it were complete). Output: a list of conversation records, each with a start/end timestamp into the shift audio and a diarized transcript, ready for scoring. This stage is the one most dependent on diarization quality — see the open questions table for where that stands."},{"p":"Architecture","f":"architecture.html","h":"score","t":"Scoring pipeline","lv":2,"k":"Each segmented conversation is sent to an LLM along with the store's rubric. The model returns a score per rubric item, a short justification, and a supporting quote from the transcript — never a bare number. The exact prompt structure and output schema are in Data Model & API. Scoring is asynchronous and can happen as soon as a conversation is segmented — it does not wait for the whole shift to end. A conversation with too little audio, or where the customer's side is inaudible, should be flagged as unscoreable rather than forced through the rubric. A confident-looking wrong score is worse than an honest gap. Per-associate daily scores are an aggregate of the day's scoreable conversations, computed after the last one lands."},{"p":"Architecture","f":"architecture.html","h":"deliver","t":"Feedback delivery","lv":2,"k":"Once the daily aggregate and message are generated, delivery goes through the app and, pending WhatsApp Business API template approval, WhatsApp — see the feedback rules in the PRD for tone and content constraints. Manager dashboards are a weekly pull, computed from the same underlying scores, not a separate pipeline."},{"p":"Architecture","f":"architecture.html","h":"failure","t":"Failure handling","lv":2,"k":"The system will fail in ordinary ways constantly — dropped uploads, a third-party API timeout, a phone dying at 40% battery. None of these should be surprising in production. FailureBehaviour Chunk upload failsStays in local buffer, retried with backoff on the next window. Nothing is deleted from device until upload is confirmed. Voicemaker request times out or errorsRequeued with backoff; after N failures, the chunk is marked failed and surfaced in an internal ops view rather than silently dropped. Segmentation finds no clear boundariesFalls back to treating the whole chunk as one long conversation rather than discarding it, flagged as low-confidence. Scoring LLM output fails schema validationOne retry with the same input; if it fails again, the conversation is marked unscored, not silently zeroed. Associate ends shift mid-uploadAny buffered chunk still uploads; end-of-shift does not ca"},{"p":"Architecture","f":"architecture.html","h":"security","t":"Security & data handling","lv":2,"k":"Row-level security in Postgres scopes every query to the associate's own data or the manager's own store — enforced at the database layer, not just in application code. Audio in transit and at rest is encrypted (TLS for upload, storage-provider encryption at rest). PII stripping runs on transcripts before long-term storage — phone numbers, names, card numbers, addresses — per the PRD's privacy commitments. Retention: transcripts deleted after 90 days by a scheduled job; whether raw audio is kept at all is still an open decision — see open questions. Access: nobody browses raw recordings by default. Playback is gated behind an explicit dispute-review flow, logged when used."},{"p":"Architecture","f":"architecture.html","h":"cost","t":"Cost & scale math","lv":2,"k":"Figures marked &dagger; are placeholders They were drafted to make the shape of the problem concrete, not derived from measurement or agreed with the customer. Nothing marked &dagger; should be quoted externally or planned against until it has been checked. Hover any marker to see this note again. Rough numbers for a single-store pilot, to sanity-check before committing to infrastructure choices. ~11 MB&dagger;Audio per associate per shift8h · Opus 16 kbps mono ~1.1 GB&dagger;Audio per 100 associate-shiftsroughly one store-week 15–25&dagger;Scoring calls per associate/dayone per conversation ~100 MBVoicemaker payload ceilingour chunks sit at ~2-3% of it At single-store pilot scale, none of storage, transcription, or LLM cost is the constraint — engineering time is. Revisit this section seriously once a rollout spans dozens of stores, where transcription and LLM scoring cost per conversat"},{"p":"Architecture","f":"architecture.html","h":"open","t":"Open technical questions","lv":2,"k":"Full list with owners and target dates lives in the roadmap's open questions table. The ones with the largest architectural blast radius: Blocking Multi-channel diarization is not live yet — segmentation and scoring both depend on it. Audio quality from a pocketed phone mic is unproven — see Week 0. Design-affecting Continuous vs. triggered recording changes the upload and battery model. Audio retention decision changes storage cost and the security surface materially. Previous← Product Requirements NextData Model & API →"},{"p":"Data & API","f":"data-model.html","h":"scope","t":"Scope of this document","lv":2,"k":"System Architecture explains why these pieces exist and how they fit together. This document is the concrete shape of each piece — table columns, endpoint contracts, JSON formats — so implementation has one place to check rather than guessing from prose. Field names below are illustrative and will drift as we build; the relationships and reasoning behind them should not."},{"p":"Data & API","f":"data-model.html","h":"overview","t":"Entity overview","lv":2,"k":"Twelve entities cover the whole system. Everything else is a join or a derived view. Retailer Store Associate Shift Audio chunk Conversation Rubric item Rubric Score Retailer → Store → Associate → Shift → Audio chunk → Conversation is the main spine. Store → Rubric → Rubric item feeds Score, which references a Conversation and a Rubric item."},{"p":"Data & API","f":"data-model.html","h":"core","t":"Core tables","lv":2,"k":"retailers The customer organisation. One row per retail chain we work with. iduuid, primary key nametext data_fiduciary_contacttext — who at the retailer owns DPDP compliance for their stores stores iduuid, primary key retailer_idfk → retailers name, citytext timezonetext — needed to bucket shifts and daily feedback correctly associates iduuid, primary key store_idfk → stores display_nametext — first name / preferred name only, not full legal identity phone_numbertext, encrypted at rest — used for WhatsApp delivery consent_signed_attimestamp — no shift can start recording without this set managers iduuid, primary key store_idfk → stores auth_user_idfk → Supabase auth user"},{"p":"Data & API","f":"data-model.html","h":"audio","t":"Audio & conversation tables","lv":2,"k":"shifts iduuid, primary key associate_idfk → associates started_at, ended_attimestamp — ended_at null while shift is in progress device_battery_at_startint, nullable — without this the end reading answers nothing device_battery_at_endint, nullable — the pair gives drain per shift, the open question in the roadmap device_model, os_versiontext — OEM battery managers differ enough that drain and kill behaviour are only interpretable per device audio_chunks One row per uploaded chunk, roughly every ~2 hours per shift. iduuid, primary key shift_idfk → shifts sequence_noint — orders chunks within a shift, independent of upload arrival order storage_pathtext — see storage layout duration_secondsint recorded_at, uploaded_attimestamp — the gap between them is the upload lag we care about operationally transcription_statusenum: pending, processing, done, failed attempt_countint — retries before a c"},{"p":"Data & API","f":"data-model.html","h":"rubric-tables","t":"Rubric tables","lv":2,"k":"rubrics iduuid, primary key store_idfk → stores versionint — rubrics change; scores must reference the version they were scored against activeboolean rubric_items iduuid, primary key rubric_idfk → rubrics labeltext — e.g. \"Needs discovery\" descriptiontext — what to look for, in the retailer's own words weightnumeric — sums to 1.0 across a rubric's items"},{"p":"Data & API","f":"data-model.html","h":"scores","t":"Scores & feedback tables","lv":2,"k":"conversation_scores iduuid, primary key conversation_idfk → conversations rubric_item_idfk → rubric_items scorenumeric, 0–1 justificationtext supporting_quotetext — verbatim from the transcript, never optional scored_attimestamp model_id, prompt_versiontext — required. Without them, a score change after a prompt edit is indistinguishable from a behaviour change, and \"agreement with a human manager\" stops being measurable across time Why the model and prompt version are not optional Score-agreement with a human manager is one of the pilot's exit criteria. The moment somebody tunes the scoring prompt, every historical score becomes incomparable unless it records what produced it. Store these from the first scored conversation, not once it becomes a problem. daily_feedback iduuid, primary key associate_idfk → associates shift_idfk → shifts message_texttext — under 80 words, per the feedback"},{"p":"Data & API","f":"data-model.html","h":"storage","t":"Storage layout","lv":2,"k":"Object storage paths are namespaced by retailer and store from day one, so per-tenant access control and eventual data export are structural, not bolted on. // audio chunk retailers/{retailer_id}/stores/{store_id}/shifts/{shift_id}/chunk-{sequence_no}.opus // transcript, if retained beyond processing retailers/{retailer_id}/stores/{store_id}/conversations/{conversation_id}/transcript.txt Whether the audio path is deleted immediately after transcription or retained for a window is the open retention decision — see roadmap."},{"p":"Data & API","f":"data-model.html","h":"mobile-api","t":"Mobile → backend API","lv":2,"k":"A small, deliberately boring set of endpoints. Everything interesting happens after upload, not in the API surface itself. EndpointPurpose POST /shifts/startCreates a shift row. Fails if consent is not on file for the associate. POST /shifts/{id}/chunksMultipart upload of one Opus chunk plus sequence number. POST /shifts/{id}/endMarks ended_at, flushes any buffered chunk uploads. GET /associates/{id}/feedback/latestIn-app read of the most recent daily feedback message."},{"p":"Data & API","f":"data-model.html","h":"voicemaker-api","t":"Backend → Voicemaker API","lv":2,"k":"Called from a Supabase edge function, triggered on chunk upload. Sends the Opus audio and requests diarized output. Payload is capped by Voicemaker at roughly 100 MB per request — our ~2-3 MB chunks leave large headroom, which is intentional; see upload & chunking for why chunk size is small by design rather than by necessity. Multi-channel diarization is not enabled on Voicemaker's side yet — until it is, responses carry text without a reliable speaker label, which blocks segmentation and scoring."},{"p":"Data & API","f":"data-model.html","h":"rubric-format","t":"Rubric JSON format","lv":2,"k":"What a retailer's rubric looks like on the wire — human-editable, versioned, weights summing to 1.0. { \"store_id\": \"...\", \"version\": 1, \"items\": [ { \"label\": \"Greeting\", \"description\": \"Associate speaks first, within ~30s, with a warm opening rather than 'yes?'\", \"weight\": 0.10 }, { \"label\": \"Needs discovery\", \"description\": \"Asks about use case, budget, or who the product is for before recommending\", \"weight\": 0.25 } ] } See what \"good\" means for why this is retailer-authored rather than something we impose."},{"p":"Data & API","f":"data-model.html","h":"prompt","t":"Scoring prompt structure","lv":2,"k":"The scoring call takes a diarized transcript and the active rubric, and must return structured output — never freeform prose, so the app can render scores and quotes reliably. // input { \"transcript\": \"[Associate] ... [Customer] ... [Associate] ...\", \"rubric\": { /* as above */ } } // required output shape, one entry per rubric item { \"item_scores\": [ { \"label\": \"Needs discovery\", \"score\": 0.8, \"justification\": \"Asked about intended use before recommending a model\", \"quote\": \"what will you mainly be using the phone for?\" } ], \"scoreable\": true, \"unscoreable_reason\": null } unscoreable_reason exists so the model has an explicit way to say \"the customer's side is inaudible\" or \"this conversation is too short to judge\" rather than forcing scores onto thin evidence. Output is validated against this schema; a response that fails validation is retried once, then marked unscored — see failure ha"},{"p":"Data & API","f":"data-model.html","h":"retention","t":"Retention & deletion jobs","lv":2,"k":"Transcripts: a scheduled job deletes conversations.transcript content 90 days after creation, per the PRD's privacy commitments. Aggregate scores are unaffected — they do not reference transcript text. Raw audio: whether audio_chunks objects are deleted immediately after successful transcription, or retained for some window, is still open — see the roadmap. The schema supports either without migration: deletion is a storage-layer job, not a schema change. PII stripping: runs as part of the transcript write, before conversations.transcript is persisted — phone numbers, names, card numbers, addresses are redacted at write time, not after the fact. Unresolved: deletion versus disputes Transcripts delete at 90 days, but a dispute is argued from the transcript. A dispute raised at day 88 and reviewed at day 92 has nothing left to review. Two plausible fixes — freeze deletion while a dispute i"},{"p":"Roadmap","f":"roadmap.html","h":"read","t":"How to read this","lv":2,"k":"This roadmap is sequenced around de-risking the two things that are unproven, not around building features. If bet 1 or bet 2 from the overview turns out false, most of what comes after Phase 1 is wasted work — so we spend real time confirming them before building anything expensive."},{"p":"Roadmap","f":"roadmap.html","h":"week0","t":"Week 0 — prove the mic works","lv":2,"k":"Before writing pipeline code, spend two days recording real store audio on an ordinary phone, in a pocket and on a lanyard, and listening to it. This is the cheapest possible test of the single riskiest assumption in the product. What we do Record 3–4 real associate-customer conversations at a live or friendly store Try phone in shirt pocket vs. on a lanyard at chest height Manually transcribe a sample and compare against what Voicemaker returns Note background noise conditions: quiet showroom vs. busy floor What tells us to stop or continue Continue as planned if the customer's side is intelligible to a human listener in most recordings. Revisit mic placement (lanyard, not pocket) if pocket audio is bad but lanyard audio is fine. Escalate to external mic / Phase 2 hardware if even lanyard audio can't reliably capture the customer. Also verify in this window: Android battery-manager kill"},{"p":"Roadmap","f":"roadmap.html","h":"phase1","t":"Phase 1 — single-store pilot","lv":2,"k":"Phone mic only, one store, small enough that we can watch it closely and cheap enough to be wrong about parts of it. In scope React Native app: start/pause/end shift, chunked upload Supabase backend, storage, edge functions Voicemaker transcription with multi-channel diarization Segmentation + rubric scoring pipeline Daily feedback via app and/or WhatsApp Weekly manager dashboard Hindi + English support Explicitly deferred External mics or dedicated hardware Languages beyond Hindi/English Sales attribution to conversations Live/real-time coaching Exit criteria The success metrics table in the PRD — usable-capture rate, coverage, transcription accuracy, score agreement with a human manager, and whether associates say they'd keep using it."},{"p":"Roadmap","f":"roadmap.html","h":"phase2","t":"Phase 2 — scale & hardware","lv":2,"k":"Only entered if Phase 1's exit criteria are met. Two separate expansions, not necessarily simultaneous: More stores, same store type. Tests whether the pipeline holds under real operational variance — different noise floors, different manager habits, different Wi-Fi reliability. External mic / dedicated hardware. Only if Week 0 or Phase 1 shows phone-mic audio quality is the ceiling on everything else. Adds cost and rollout friction, so only pursued if the data says the phone mic genuinely can't get there. Languages beyond Hindi and English, and sales attribution via POS integration, are Phase 2 candidates but not commitments — see what we are not doing for why each is deferred rather than ruled out."},{"p":"Roadmap","f":"roadmap.html","h":"timeline","t":"Timeline at a glance","lv":2,"k":"Week 0 — not started Audio quality spike Real-world recording test, background-recording survival check on Android. Weeks 1–2 Recording & upload Mobile app: shift lifecycle, Opus compression, chunked resumable upload. Weeks 2–3 Transcription & segmentation Voicemaker integration, multi-channel diarization live, conversation boundary detection. Weeks 3–4 Rubric & scoring Rubric v1 from the retailer, scoring pipeline, feedback message generation. Weeks 4–5 Delivery & dashboard Daily feedback delivery, weekly manager view, end-to-end dry run with internal test data. Week 6+ Live pilot Real associates, real store, watched closely against the exit criteria above. Indicative, not committed. Week numbers are relative to a start date nobody has set yet, and no phase has begun. Every phase after Week 0 assumes diarization lands and audio quality holds — see open questions below. Nothing here has "},{"p":"Roadmap","f":"roadmap.html","h":"open","t":"Open questions","lv":2,"k":"Referenced from the overview and the PRD. Every item needs an owner and a target date before Phase 1 can be called complete. QuestionOwnerTarget Do we keep audio after transcription, or only the transcript?SabariBefore Phase 1 build starts Continuous vs. triggered recording — always-on, or associate taps to start each conversation?SabariEnd of Week 0 Consent mechanism for the customer — signage alone, or something explicit?YatinBefore pilot store go-live Feedback channel — in-app, WhatsApp, or both from day one?SabariWeeks 3–4 Diarization quality — unproven until Voicemaker multi-channel is liveSujitWithin 1 week Dialect accuracy — Punjabi-accented Hindi is a known gap, untestedSujitDuring Weeks 2–3 Scoring transparency — does the associate see the transcript behind a score?Sabari + YatinWeeks 3–4 Battery cost of an 8-hour recording session, unmeasuredSabariEnd of Week 0 Transcript delet"},{"p":"Roadmap","f":"roadmap.html","h":"risks","t":"Risk register","lv":2,"k":"Stated plainly, matching the failure modes in the PRD but framed for tracking rather than narrative. RiskLikelihoodImpactMitigation Phone mic can't reliably capture the customerLow confidence it worksCriticalWeek 0 spike; fallback to lanyard placement or external mic Associates stop running the appMediumHighMake pausing frictionless and never penalised; monitor coverage weekly Scores feel wrong to associates, trust collapsesMediumHighAlways show the supporting quote; scoring transparency decision above Scores get used punitively despite commitmentsMediumCriticalContractual commitment with retailer; visible in onboarding materials Diarization not ready in timeMediumHighSujit's one-week target; blocks segmentation and scoring if missed Feedback is accurate but behaviour doesn't changeMediumMediumOnly answerable by running the pilot long enough to see score trends"},{"p":"Roadmap","f":"roadmap.html","h":"next","t":"Owner-by-owner next steps","lv":2,"k":"PersonOwnsImmediate next step Sabari System architecture, mobile app, backend pipeline Run the Week 0 audio spike, then start the React Native recording build Sujit Transcription stack, Voicemaker integration Enable multi-channel diarization; share API docs with Sabari Yatin Customer relationship, rubric definition, pilot site Set up the three-way group; get rubric v1 from the retailer; scope the consent mechanism This is a living document Dates here are planning targets, not commitments to the customer. Update this page as Week 0 results come in — if bet 1 turns out wrong, the whole sequencing above changes, and that should show up here first. Previous← Data Model & API NextBack to Overview →"}];
